---
// Hand-authored SVG: the "one Jacobian, three consumers" architecture diagram.
// R(u,p) -> J = dR/du, consumed by Newton, EIS and the adjoint. See /numerics/.
---

<figure class="jacobian-figure">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 760 340"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="jac-title jac-desc"
    class="jacobian-svg"
  >
    <title id="jac-title">One Jacobian, three consumers</title>
    <desc id="jac-desc">
      The architecture planned for the Python package, not something this site runs. The
      residual R of u and p is differentiated once to give the Jacobian J, and that single
      matrix feeds three consumers: the damped Newton solver for steady states, the
      frequency-domain impedance solve, and the adjoint equation for design sensitivities.
    </desc>

    <defs>
      <marker id="jac-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
        <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="currentColor"></path>
      </marker>
    </defs>

    <!-- residual box -->
    <g class="jac-box">
      <rect x="20" y="130" width="170" height="80" rx="6"></rect>
      <text x="105" y="163" class="jac-math" text-anchor="middle">R(u, p) = 0</text>
      <text x="105" y="190" class="jac-label" text-anchor="middle">discretised residual</text>
    </g>

    <!-- differentiation arrow -->
    <line x1="190" y1="170" x2="280" y2="170" class="jac-edge" marker-end="url(#jac-arrow)"></line>
    <text x="235" y="156" class="jac-label" text-anchor="middle">linearise</text>

    <!-- Jacobian box -->
    <g class="jac-box jac-box-accent">
      <rect x="282" y="130" width="150" height="80" rx="6"></rect>
      <text x="357" y="163" class="jac-math" text-anchor="middle">J = ∂R/∂u</text>
      <text x="357" y="190" class="jac-label" text-anchor="middle">one sparse Jacobian</text>
    </g>

    <!-- fan-out edges -->
    <path d="M432 152 C 490 120, 510 80, 552 62" class="jac-edge" fill="none" marker-end="url(#jac-arrow)"></path>
    <line x1="432" y1="170" x2="552" y2="170" class="jac-edge" marker-end="url(#jac-arrow)"></line>
    <path d="M432 188 C 490 220, 510 260, 552 278" class="jac-edge" fill="none" marker-end="url(#jac-arrow)"></path>

    <!-- consumer: Newton -->
    <g class="jac-box">
      <rect x="555" y="20" width="185" height="80" rx="6"></rect>
      <text x="647" y="50" class="jac-math" text-anchor="middle">J δu = −R</text>
      <text x="647" y="72" class="jac-label" text-anchor="middle">damped Newton</text>
      <text x="647" y="88" class="jac-label" text-anchor="middle">steady state &amp; transients</text>
    </g>

    <!-- consumer: EIS -->
    <g class="jac-box">
      <rect x="555" y="130" width="185" height="80" rx="6"></rect>
      <text x="647" y="160" class="jac-math" text-anchor="middle">(iωM − J) û = b̂</text>
      <text x="647" y="182" class="jac-label" text-anchor="middle">impedance</text>
      <text x="647" y="198" class="jac-label" text-anchor="middle">one solve per frequency</text>
    </g>

    <!-- consumer: adjoint -->
    <g class="jac-box">
      <rect x="555" y="240" width="185" height="80" rx="6"></rect>
      <text x="647" y="270" class="jac-math" text-anchor="middle">Jᵀ λ = −∂Q/∂u</text>
      <text x="647" y="292" class="jac-label" text-anchor="middle">adjoint sensitivities</text>
      <text x="647" y="308" class="jac-label" text-anchor="middle">dQ/dp for all parameters</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="demo-caption">
    The architecture designed for the Python package <code>voltaire-echem</code>: one Jacobian
    of the residual, with Newton iteration, frequency-domain impedance, and adjoint design
    sensitivities all consuming the same matrix, so linearisation is written once and tested
    once. None of this diagram is implemented — that package has no code yet, and the browser
    solver on this site hand-assembles its PNP Jacobian and has no adjoint.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .jacobian-figure {
    margin: var(--s6) 0;
  }

  .jacobian-svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }

  .jac-box rect {
    fill: var(--surface);
    stroke: var(--line-strong);
    stroke-width: 1.25;
  }

  .jac-box-accent rect {
    stroke: var(--accent);
    stroke-width: 1.75;
  }

  .jac-math {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 17px;
    fill: var(--fg);
  }

  .jac-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    /* 10.5 not 11.5: the widest consumer label is 25 characters in a 185px box,
       and at 11.5px it touched both borders. */
    font-size: 10.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    fill: var(--fg-muted);
  }

  .jac-edge {
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }
</style>
